## Issue

I recently encountered an issue where my SIFT Workstation Linux VM kept crashing when hosted on VMware Workstation via a Windows host. The VM would work fine for about five minutes, then the CPU would suddenly spike and the machine would freeze.

## Fix

The issue appeared to stem from resource utilization related to VMware Workstation running on top of Windows Hypervisor Platform instead of VMware's own hypervisor. To solve, I navigate to "Turn Windows Feature On or Off" via the search menu and uncheck the Windows Hypervisor Platform option. 

I then had to go to a cmd shell with admin privileges and type:
```
bcdedit /set hypervisorlaunchtype off 
```

Upon restarting the Windows host, the issue no longer occured and starting up the SIFT vm. 

However, during some restarts, the issue would reappear and I had to reenter the bcdedit command and restart the host. According to Gemini, this may be caused by:
- Windows Memory Integrity: In my case this did not make a difference
- Windows Virtual Machine Platform: This can also be disabled from the "Turn Windows Feature On or Off" menu, but also disables WSL2 which I needed.
- Windows Fast Startup: This did not seem to make a difference.

As such, these instructions may have to be repeated. If I find a consistent fix, I'll update this page.
